<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tatev Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tatev Monastery is a 9th-century Armenian monastery on a basalt promontory above the Vorotan gorge in Syunik Province, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Syunik, it owned many villages and in the 14th and 15th centuries became a leading centre of learning, associated with the scholar Grigor Tatevatsi. It is reached today by a long cable car, the Wings of Tatev.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tatev-monastery.jpg" alt="Tatev Monastery" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Well-read MountainMan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:-2025.05_%D0%A2%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D1%8B%D1%80%D1%8C_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tatev plateau was in use before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, when it held a pagan temple, replaced by a modest church after the Christianization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in the 4th century. Tradition derives the name from Eustateus, a disciple of Thaddeus the Apostle who preached and was martyred in the region. A folk legend instead has a builder falling into the gorge and calling &quot;Ta Tev&quot;, Armenian for &quot;give me wings&quot;. The historian Stepanos Orbelian describes a new church built beside the old one in 848 with the help of Prince Philip of Syunik, and Bishop Hovhannes later won the support of Prince Ashot of Syunik for a larger monastery.</p><p>Tatev became a feudal institution. Peasants of Tsouraberd and other villages rejected its authority, and their protests twice grew into uprisings that lasted, with interruptions, until 990, when King Vasak of Syunik razed Tsouraberd. The bishopric owned 47 villages and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> from 677 others. In 940–950 Bishop Hakob tried to secede from Etchmiadzin and was anathematised by Catholicos Anania Mokatsi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The fortified complex has three churches, a library, a refectory, a bell tower and a mausoleum. The church of Saints Paul and Peter, the oldest surviving building, was built between 895 and 906 as a domed basilica, with bas-relief portraits of Ashot and Princess Shushan on its eastern façade. </p><p>Frescoes added in 930 were painted by artists invited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> working with Armenian painters, and little survives. The Holy Mother of God Church was added in 1087, and Saint Gregory the Illuminator's was replaced in 1295 under Bishop Stepanos Orbelian. Outside the walls stands an olive press, one of the best preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and near the cathedral stands the Gavazan, a column about eight meters tall crowned by a khachkar-type cross, which sways without falling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A university operated at Tatev between 1390 and 1434. In the early 1340s Hovhan Vorotnetsi came from Gladzor and, with the patronage of the Orbelian princes, organised it in three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>: scriptures and the humanities, the arts of the book including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a>, and music. After his death in 1388 Grigor Tatevatsi became abbot, and teaching extended to the Old and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testaments</a>, the church fathers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and Porphyry.</p><p>Its scholars opposed the Fratres Unitores, an Armenian branch of the Dominican order, and helped persuade the authorities to return the supreme patriarchal throne from Sis to Etchmiadzin, achieved in 1441. The university ceased to function after the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> in 1434.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tatev Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
